  ],
  "rationale": "Genius + Soul = Jazz is the record that launched Impulse! — ABC-Paramount's second release on the new Creed Taylor imprint — and the date that fixed the organ-plus-big-band soul-jazz formula. Ray Charles plays Hammond B3 on every track, fronting two overlapping big bands drawn from Count Basie's orchestra (Freddie Green, Eddie Jones, Sonny Payne, Frank Foster, Frank Wess, Charlie Fowlkes, Snooky Young, Thad Jones, Joe Newman, Henry Coker, Al Grey, Benny Powell) and from New York's first-call session players (Clark Terry, Urbie Green, Jimmy Cleveland, Joe Wilder, Jimmy Nottingham, Roy Haynes, Joe Benjamin, Budd Johnson, Seldon Powell, Sam Herman), with brass and reed charts by Quincy Jones and Ralph Burns [S2, S3, S8] (obs). Consensus signals: inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame in 2011 and voted No. 360 in Colin Larkin's All Time Top 1000 Albums [S2] (obs); AllMusic's own page files it under Soul Jazz and Jazz Blues, and the review-score table records AllMusic at 4.5/5 [S1, S2] (obs); Jazz Journal's 2023 review closes by awarding it five ('It's straight-ahead stuff, nothing innovative, but just good, solid jazz and blues, played by masters') [S9] (obs). Chart evidence in the record itself: No. 4 on Billboard's Top LPs in the issue of 2 April 1961, in a forty-eight-week run [S2] (obs). Session gravity: the 26 December ensemble IS the Basie rhythm section with Charles at the organ; the 27 December ensemble is Basie's bookends plus New York's first-call brass, and both sessions are documented with per-track assignments and per-instrument splits [S2, S3] (obs). Discovery value: 'One Mint Julep' — Charles comping behind the band instead of singing over it — is the two-minute argument for the whole record, and the album is the load-bearing link between the Basie records already in this canon and the soul-jazz organ tradition that follows [S1, S2, S9] (inf). Scope note: recorded December 1960, released February 1961 — squarely inside the 1945–1985 window; style code soul-jazz follows AllMusic's own classification of the record (its primary genre listing is R&B/Blues, styles include Soul Jazz and Jazz Blues) [S1] (obs).",
