    "notes": "Two sessions, eight days before the decade turned: 26 December 1960 (tracks 1, 2, 3, 7, 8, 9 — LP sides A1-A3, B2-B4) and 27 December 1960 (tracks 4, 5, 6, 10 — A4, A5, B1, B5). The per-session split is consistent across Wikipedia (S2), Discogs' track-range credits (S3) and Jazz Journal (S9). PRODUCER/ENGINEER: Creed Taylor is credited as producer by every source (S1, S3, S7, S8) — that claim is obs. No source names an engineer; the engineer entry 'Rudy Van Gelder' is the contract's documented inference for a Van Gelder Studio date of this period, hence epistemic_production 'inf' rather than 'obs'. STUDIO CONFLICT, preserved: Wikipedia (S2), All About Jazz (S6), the Rock Hall catalog record (S7) and the session notes at the Ray Charles Video Museum (S8, with Creed Taylor describing Rudy Van Gelder doctoring the Hammond's keys for the date) all place the sessions at Van Gelder Studio, Englewood Cliffs; Jazz Journal's discography prints 'NYC, 26, 27 December 1960' (S9). Four sources to one, and the Creed Taylor account is first-hand, so the site is recorded as Van Gelder Studio with epistemic 'obs'; the Jazz Journal wording is the dissent, not a co-equal claim. Ray Charles plays organ on all ten tracks and sings on two ('I've Got News for You', 'I'm Gonna Move to the Outskirts of Town') — that singing split is Discogs' (S3) and Jazz Journal's (S9) shared account. Wikipedia and AllMusic also credit him with piano (S1, S2); Discogs' instrument-by-instrument credit list names only organ (S3), and no source assigns piano to a track, so piano is not carried as an instrument row. PERSONNEL UNION: 32 players across the two bands, matching the count on the album's credit summary (S3, S10). AllMusic (S1) carries two credits that do not match the other sources: 'Dick Katz — Piano' and 'Eugene Young — Trumpet'. The Discogs credit list has Dick Katz as the liner-note writer, and jazzcodex's credit summary agrees (Liner Notes, Sleeve Notes); 'Eugene Young' is Snooky Young, who is on the 26 December band. Dick Katz is therefore NOT recorded as a pianist here, and the AllMusic piano credit is flagged rather than copied. ARRANGERS, conflicting and unresolved: Wikipedia's track table (S2) and the session notes at the Ray Charles Video Museum (S8) credit Quincy Jones on tracks 1, 3, 5, 6, 9 and Ralph Burns on 2, 4, 7, 8, 10; Discogs' per-track arranger credits (S3) reverse the last three, giving Jones 1, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8 and Burns 2, 4, 9, 10. Arranger is not a personnel role in this record's taxonomy, so the disagreement is recorded here and not resolved. TAKES AND REISSUES: the original Impulse! LP is ten tracks (36:14 per Wikipedia, S2; AllMusic prints 37:06, S1 — variance noted, Wikipedia's timings recorded). Later editions add three 23 June 1959 vocal selections outside this record's two sessions (S9); they are not carried. TRACK ORDER: sides as on the original LP (A1-A5, B1-B5)."
